Well, according to my reading of the code, monsters can never be
generated carrying such things.  Aside from weapons, armor, and "normal"
class-based equipment (bugles for soldiers, gold for leprechauns, wands
of striking/magic missile, keys, and healing potions for shopkeepers,
mummy wrappings for mummys, and stuff like that), a monster can either
get a rnd_offensive_item(), a rnd_defensive_item(), a rnd_misc_item(),
or gold.  Offensive items are wands of striking, magic missile, sleep,
fire, cold, lightning, or death; potions of acid, confusion, blindness,
sleeping, paralysis; and scrolls of earth.  Defensive items are wands of
teleportation, create monster, or digging; scrolls of teleportation or
create monster; potions of healing, extra healing, full healing, or
sickness (Pestilence only).  Miscellaneous items are wands of polymorph,
speed monster or make invisible; potions of polymorph, speed, or
invisibility; and amulets of life saving.  Those are all the items a
monster can have in its inventory when it is generated.

However, there are a couple other monster-related ways items can be
generated.  Spiders and snakes will have a random object created for
them to hide under when they are generated.  And there is a chance of a
random object appearing when a monster is killed.  So, yes, it is
possible to get any object by killing a monster (as long as it can leave
a corpse, and there are limitations on what you can get based on size).
 However, the monster was not generated with that object and was never
carrying it.  Minor difference maybe, but it does have real effects
(e.g. the object cannot be used/stolen/destroyed while the monster is
still alive).

Perhaps I answered the original question too narrowly, but I was only
looking at the carrying-when-generated part of the code at the time.

> What the chances are i do not know, but they are rather small and i
> _think_ monster level factors into it. A full-scale extinction seems
> to net two or three magic markers, so don't count on it.

Monster level does not appear to factor into the random-item-upon-death
case (the code that generates the object does not know the monster
level).  Specific dungeon level doesn't either; however, there are
special probabilities for the rogue level and Gehennom.  The "good"
stuff is more likely in Gehennom, so it may be better to do your killing
there.  The probabilities only affect object class, not whether you will
get a better item within a given class.

